afety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":4,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I'm approving PKT-0114: the port does what the goal asks, stays within the contract and is well under budget.\n\n**Goal.** In `DocService/sources/routes/static.js`, both error paths (the `sendFile` callback and the S3/Azure `catch`) now send a 400 only when `!res.headersSent`. A client that disconnects is logged at debug level instead of as an error. `aiProxyHandler.js` gets the same `headersSent` check, and sending the error response is wrapped in a try/catch so a failure there can't throw out of the handler. Leaving out the first upstream hunk is fine: it only rewords a comment and the log explains why.\n\n**Test.** `tests/unit/staticAbort.tests.js` checks real behaviour, not a stand-in:\n- It runs the real router on a local test server and switches the storage backend to S3, which is the path that called `sendStatus(400)` before the fix.\n- It checks the 200 arrives with the first chunk, then aborts the client.\n- It then checks that `sendStatus` is never called and that the source stream is destroyed.\n- The executor says it fails on the old code. That fits: an abort after headers went out would reach the old `catch`, which called `sendStatus(400)`.\n\n**Contract.** No existing test assertions changed. Three code files are touched against a budget of six, and about 140 diff lines against 800.\n\n**Bookkeeping.** The Executor log is filled in and includes the decisions, the surprises and the lesson for the Atlas. The friction line is there. No follow-up packets were requested, so the empty Spawned packets section is consistent.\n\n**Non-blocking remarks:**\n- In `static.js`, the S3/Azure `catch` only treats `ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE` as a disconnect. The `sendFile` path also covers `ECONNRESET` and `ECONNABORTED`. A reset there will still be logged as an error, but no status is sent after headers, so the behaviour that matters is right. This follows upstream.\n- 